                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                = succeed "-monad" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibility"} possibility
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "#JohnsonRosebrughWood10"}> Johnson, Rosebrugh &amp; Wood 2010, Prop. 9
                , see Prop.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LawfulLensesArePossibilityModales"}
                below)
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comodule+over+a+comonad"} comodales
                over the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/costate+comonad"} costate comonad
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "#O’Connor10"}> O’Connor (2010)
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#O’Connor11"} (2011)
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LensesAreCostateCoalgebras"}
                below).
          %p
            Thus, lawful lenses may be thought of as encoding read/write functionality (only) at a certain address in a data base (compare the
            = succeed "-model" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RAM"} RAM
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+monad"} state monad
            which encodes read/write of the entire data base type at once).
          %p More generally, there are two different approaches to lenses:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Lawful lenses (as above) are an algebraic structure which axiomatize product projections. The laws govern the ways views and updates relate. These are generalized into
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta+lens"} Delta lenses
            %li
              %p
                Lawless lenses, and in particular bi-directional or polymorphic lenses, separate the two directions of view and update so that the laws no longer type-check. These lawless lenses are used to organize bi-directional flow of data, and are generalized into
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/optics+%28in+computer+science%29"} optics
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+programming"} functional programming
                literature.
          %p
            An alternate generalization of lawless lenses is put forward in
            %a{:href => "#Spivak19"} Spivak 19
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"} Grothendieck construction
            of the fiberwise dual of an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+category"} indexed category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+systems+theory"} categorical systems theory
            because they represent a
            %em bidirectional stateful computation
            which describes the way some systems expose and update their internal state. For instance, see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Myers"} Myers
            %a{:href => "#SpivakPoly"} Spivak &amp; Niu
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Hedges21"} Hedges (2021)
          %p
            In this sense, lawless lenses are applications of two mathematical frameworks which are interesting in their own right:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration"} fibrations
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/optics+%28in+computer+science%29"} optics
            (thus
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tambara+module"} Tambara modules
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p
          #Lens.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (lenses)
              %br/
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{C}, 1, \times)
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
              %em lens
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
              is:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p (“source”)
                    .maruku-equation
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi S
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo ∈
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi C
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \,\in\, \mathbf{C}
                  %li
                    %p (“view”)
                    .maruku-equation
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi V
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo ∈
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi C
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \,\in\, \mathbf{C}
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                  of the form
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p (“view”, “read-out”, “forward operation”)
                    .maruku-equation
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} get
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi S
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mi V
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                            \\mathrm{get}
                            \\;\colon\;
                            S \longrightarrow V
                  %li
                    %p (“update”, “write”, “backward operation”)
                    .maruku-equation
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} put
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi V
                            %mo ×
                            %mi S
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mi S
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo .
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                            \\mathrm{put}
                            \\;\colon\;
                            V \times S \longrightarrow S
                            \\,.
            %p
              The lens is called
              %em well-behaved
              if this data satisfies:
            %ul
              %li
                %p (PutGet) “what has just been written is read out as such”:
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi V
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi S
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi get
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                        %mi put
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi v
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo =
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi v
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        v \colon V
                        ,\,
                        s \colon S
                        \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                        \\vdash
                        \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                        get\big(
                        put
                        (v,\,s)
                        \\big)
                        \\;=\;
                        v
                        \\,,
            %p and
            %ul
              %li
                %p (GetPut) “writing what has just been read out means no change”:
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi S
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi put
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                        %mi get
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo =
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi s
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        s \colon S
                        \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                        \\vdash
                        \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                        put\big(
                        get(s)
                        ,\,
                        s
                        \\big)
                        \\;=\;
                        s
            %p
              and it is called
              %em very well-behaved
              or
              %em lawful
              if in addition it satisfies:
            %ul
              %li
                %p (PutPut) “writing means to over-write previous edits”:
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi v
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi v
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi V
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi S
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi put
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} (
                        %mi v
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi put
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                        %mi v
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo =
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi put
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi v
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        v,\,v' \colon V
                        ,\;
                        s \colon S
                        \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                        \\vdash
                        \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                        put\Big(
                        v',\,
                        put\big(
                        v,\, s
                        \\big)
                        \\Big)
                        \\;=\;
                        put(v',\, s)
            %p
          %p
            Definition
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Lens"}
            appears, under no particular name, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HofmannPierce96"} Hofmann &amp; Pierce 1996, p. 12
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object-oriented+programming"} object-oriented programming
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            as a “class” which inherits functionality from the class
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#PossibilityMonadicity"} below
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi D
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \simeq D \times V
            is a “record” which inherits all fields present in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            but may add some more).
          %p
            The terminology “lens” for Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Lens"}
            is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BohannonPierceVaughan06"} Bohannon, Pierce &amp; Vaughan 2006, Def. 3.2
          %h2#the_category_of_lenses The category of lenses
          %p
            Lenses (regardless of their lawfulness) organize in a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Lens
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Lens}(\mathbf C)
            whose objects are the same as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf C
            and whose morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
            are lenses with states
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and views
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1_X, \pi_1) :X \to X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} get
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} put
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathrm{get}_1, \mathrm{put}_{1}):X \to Y
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} get
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} put
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathrm{get}_2, \mathrm{put}_{2}):Y \to Z
            is given by:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} get
                    %mn 12
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} get
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} get
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathrm{get}_{12} = \mathrm{get}_2 \circ \mathrm{get}_1
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} put
                    %mn 12
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} put
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} put
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi Z
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} get
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi Z
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathrm{put}_{12} = \mathrm{put}_1 \circ ((\mathrm{put}_2 \circ (1_Z \times \mathrm{get}_1)) \times 1_X) \circ (1_Z \times \Delta_X)
          %p
            The
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} put
                    %mn 12
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{put}_{12}
            morphism is probably easier to describe using generalized elements:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} put
                    %mn 12
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} put
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} put
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} get
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathrm{put}_{12} : (z,x) \mapsto \mathrm{put}_1(\mathrm{put}_2(z, \mathrm{get}_1(x)), x)
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Crucially, associativity of this composition relies on
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category+with+diagonals"} naturality of the diagonals
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/optic+%28in+computer+science%29"} Optics
              are a sweeping generalization of lenses which overcomes this obstacle.
          %p
            Moreover, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf C
            endows
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Lens
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Lens}(\mathbf{C})
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+product"} monoidal product
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#PossibilityMonadicity Monadicity over Possibility monad
          %p
          #LawfulLensesArePossibilityModales.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (lawful lenses are possibility modales)
              %br/
              Lawful lenses in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
              with view
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              (Def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Lens"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+of+a+monad"} modules
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modales"}> modales
              ) of the left
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
