            %p
          This is due to
          = succeed "," do
            %a{:href => "#O’Connor10"} O’Connor (2010)
          = succeed "," do
            %a{:href => "#O’Connor11"} O’Connor (2011)
          = succeed "." do
            %a{:href => "#GibbonsJohnson12"} Gibbons &amp; Johnson (2012), Section 3.2
          %p
            A
            %strong proof
            is spelled out
            %a{:href => "store+comonad#LabelStoreComodalesAreLenses"} here
            at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/store+comonad"} store comonad
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            There are many generalizations of lenses which have been proposed, however they can be broadly classified into those which satisfy analogues of the
            = succeed "," do
              %em lens laws
          %h3#lenses_without_laws Lenses without laws
          %ul
            %li
              One generalization considers the lenses from the previous section as
              %em monomorphic
              by contrast to
              %em polymorphic
              lens which go between pairs of types,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda: (S, T) \to (A, B)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi v
                        %mi λ
                      %mo :
                      %mi S
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_{\lambda}: S \to A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi u
                        %mi λ
                      %mo :
                      %mi S
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u_{\lambda}: S \times B \to T
              = succeed "." do
                %em bimorphic lenses
          %p
            These sorts of lenses are generalized by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Spivak19"} Spivak 19
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Myers20"} Myers20
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Myers"} Myers
          %ul
            %li
              An
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/optic+%28in+computer+science%29"} optic
              generalizes the way lenses ‘remember’ state from the forward part to the backward part, avoiding the necessity of a cartesian structure by swapping it with a sufficiently rich actegorical context.
          %h3#lenses_with_laws Lenses with laws
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta+lens"} Delta lenses
                are a generalization which does satisfy laws. Here we have categories
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                called the source and view, together with a
                %em Get
                functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi S
                      %mo →
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : S \to V
                and a function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mn 0
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 0
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi \colon S_{0} \times_{V_{0}} V_{1} \to S_{1}
                which takes a pair
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi u
                      %mo :
                      %mi g
                      %mi s
                      %mo →
                      %mi v
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (s \in S, u : g s \to v \in V)
                to a morphism
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo ,
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi s
                      %mo →
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo ,
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi(s, u) : s \to p(s, u)
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo ,
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi cod
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(s, u) = cod(\varphi(a, u))
                is the
                %em Put
                function. The function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
                must also satisfy three lens laws. When
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                are
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indiscrete+category"} codiscrete categories
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#JohnsonRosebrugh16"} Johnson-Rosebrugh 2016, Proposition 4
            %li
              %p
                A morphism between directed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+functor"} containers
                is another kind of generalised lens satisfying laws called
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em update-update lenses
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#AhmanUustalu17"} Ahman-Uustalu 2017, Section 5
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofunctor"} cofunctors
